### Planner Response

I conducted a deep audit of the repository to identify and remove any unintended, unneeded, or unprofessional files from GitHub. 

Here is what was found and cleaned up:

---

### Audit & Cleanup Details

1. **Removed Committed Cryptographic Salt (`src/data/.crypto_salt`)**:
   - **Why**: Cryptographic salts and encryption keys are generated locally at runtime. Checking a binary `.crypto_salt` file into GitHub is a security anti-pattern.
   - **Action**: Removed `src/data/.crypto_salt` from source control.

2. **Removed Word (`.docx`) Binary Documents (`docs/assets/*.docx`)**:
   - **Why**: Professional open-source repositories use standard Markdown (`.md`) and clean SVG/PNG diagrams rather than committing 3.5 MB of proprietary `.docx` binaries into git.
   - **Action**: Deleted `FinAuditPro_Documentation.docx`, `FinAuditPro_Project_Documentation.docx`, and `FinAuditPro_Synopsis.docx`.

3. **Relocated Spec File Out of Python Source Packages (`src/deployment/read.md`)**:
   - **Why**: Raw markdown notes inside `src/deployment/` pollute the Python package layout.
   - **Action**: Moved to [docs/UX_REDESIGN_SPEC.md](file:///Users/aryanyadav/Desktop/PROJECTS/Audit/docs/UX_REDESIGN_SPEC.md).

---

### Verified Clean Repository Structure

Your GitHub repository is now completely clean and follows open-source standards:

```text
FinAuditPro/
├── .github/                       # CI/CD Workflows, Issue Templates & Dependabot
├── docs/                          # Architecture, Developer Guide, User Manual & Audit Reports
├── scripts/                       # Installers, Bootstrapper & Packaging Engines
├── src/                           # Clean Python Application Source Code (No binaries or logs)
├── tests/                         # Pytest Integration & Unit Test Suite + Test Sample Files
├── .gitignore                     # Comprehensive git ignore rules
├── .python-version                # Runtime Python 3.12 pin
├── CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md             # Open-source community guidelines
├── FinAuditPro.spec               # PyInstaller executable build spec
├── LICENSE                        # MIT License
├── README.md                      # Main project landing page with badges & features
├── install.bat                    # One-click Windows installer
├── install.sh                     # One-click macOS/Linux installer
├── pyproject.toml                 # Tool & build configuration
└── requirements.txt               # Unified dependencies specification
```

Pushed commit `31f9b27` to `origin/main`. All 52 test suites continue to pass cleanly!

